/* CAGR Club curriculum: eight long lessons in four archives.
   Each lesson: t, min, sum, key, secs [[heading, html]], w (widget), ex (worked example), cq/ca (check yourself),
   papers [{t, a, y, src, u, why}], videos [{t, ch, id, why}], links [{t, src, u, why}].
   Companies in examples are invented. */
var DEEP=[
{t:'The three statements, and how they talk to each other',min:22,w:'pnl',
 sum:'The profit and loss statement, the balance sheet and the cash flow statement, line by line, and the links between them that every analyst checks.',
 key:'Each statement answers a different question. Profit tells you how the year went, the balance sheet tells you where the company stands, and cash flow tells you whether the money actually arrived. A story that holds in one but not the others is the first thing to investigate.',
 secs:[
 ['Why there are three statements, not one',`<p>A company could, in principle, publish one number: how much cash it had at the end of the year. That number would be honest and almost useless. It would not tell you whether the company earned that cash, borrowed it, or sold a factory to get it.</p><p>So accounting splits the picture three ways. The <b>profit and loss statement</b> (P&amp;L) measures performance over a period, using accrual accounting: revenue is counted when it is earned, not when the customer pays, and costs are matched to the revenue they helped produce. The <b>balance sheet</b> is a photograph of what the company owns and owes on one date. The <b>cash flow statement</b> reconciles the two, showing how the cash balance moved.</p><p>The reason this matters for an analyst is that each statement can be flattered, but rarely all three at once. Aggressive revenue recognition lifts profit, and then shows up as receivables piling up on the balance sheet and weak operating cash flow. That cross-check is most of what this archive teaches.</p>`],
 ['The P&L, line by line',`<p><b>Revenue from operations</b> comes first. Under Ind AS 115, revenue is recognised when control of the goods or service passes to the customer, and Indian companies report it net of GST. Look at the revenue note, not just the headline: it splits sale of products, sale of services and other operating revenue such as export incentives or scrap sales.</p><p>Below revenue sit the costs. <b>Cost of materials consumed</b> and changes in inventory give you gross profit and gross margin, the cleanest read on pricing power. <b>Employee benefits expense</b> and <b>other expenses</b> (power, freight, advertising, royalty) take you to <b>EBITDA</b>, the profit the operations generate before the cost of the capital used to build them.</p><p><b>Depreciation and amortisation</b> spreads the cost of assets over their lives; subtracting it gives <b>EBIT</b>, or operating profit. <b>Finance costs</b> are interest. <b>Other income</b> is usually interest on cash and treasury gains; keep it separate, because a company that earns half its profit on its bank balance is a fund, not an operating business. <b>Exceptional items</b> are one-offs the company wants you to ignore; check how often they recur.</p><p>Tax is next. India’s corporate tax rate under the concessional regime is 25.17% including surcharge and cess, but the effective rate in the accounts can differ because of deferred tax, exempt income or units under older regimes. Profit after tax, less the share belonging to minority shareholders, divided by the weighted number of shares, gives <b>earnings per share</b>, basic and diluted.</p>`],
 ['The balance sheet: what it owns, what it owes',`<p>The balance sheet always balances: assets equal liabilities plus equity. On the asset side, <b>property, plant and equipment</b> is the factory and machinery at cost less accumulated depreciation. <b>Capital work in progress</b> (CWIP) is spending on assets not yet in use. A CWIP balance that stays large for years is worth a question: either the project is late, or costs are being parked there instead of hitting the P&amp;L. <b>Right-of-use assets</b> are leased premises, brought onto the balance sheet by Ind AS 116 along with a matching <b>lease liability</b>. <b>Goodwill</b> is the premium paid in past acquisitions over the fair value of what was bought.</p><p>Current assets are the working parts: <b>inventories</b>, <b>trade receivables</b> (money customers owe) and <b>cash and bank balances</b>. Current liabilities include <b>trade payables</b> (money owed to suppliers), short-term borrowings and provisions.</p><p>Equity is share capital plus <b>other equity</b>, mostly retained earnings. Net debt is borrowings, including lease liabilities if you are consistent about it, minus cash and liquid investments. Read the <b>contingent liabilities</b> note too: tax disputes and guarantees that are not on the balance sheet yet can be larger than the debt that is.</p>`],
 ['The cash flow statement: where the money really went',`<p>Indian companies almost always use the <b>indirect method</b>. It starts from profit before tax and adds back non-cash charges such as depreciation, removes non-operating items such as interest income, and then adjusts for <b>changes in working capital</b>: if receivables rose by ₹50 crore, that is profit you booked but did not collect, so it is subtracted. Taxes actually paid come off at the end. The result is <b>cash flow from operations</b> (CFO).</p><p><b>Investing cash flow</b> is mostly capital expenditure and purchases or sales of investments. <b>Financing cash flow</b> is borrowing, repayment, interest paid, dividends and share issues. Under Ind AS, non-financial companies usually show interest paid under financing and interest received under investing, which matters when you compare with companies that classify differently.</p><p>The figure analysts care about most is <b>free cash flow</b>. The simple version is CFO minus capex. For valuation you will use two cleaner versions: <b>free cash flow to the firm</b> (cash available to all capital providers, before interest) and <b>free cash flow to equity</b> (after interest and net borrowing). The DCF lesson builds on these.</p>`],
 ['One transaction, three statements',`<p>Trace a single event and the links become obvious. Suppose a company buys a machine for ₹100 crore in cash at the start of the year and depreciates it over ten years.</p><p>On the day of purchase, nothing happens to profit. On the balance sheet, cash falls by 100 and PP&amp;E rises by 100. On the cash flow statement, investing cash flow shows −100.</p><p>By year end, depreciation of 10 has gone through the P&amp;L. With a 25% tax rate, profit after tax falls by 7.5 and the company pays 2.5 less tax. On the cash flow statement, operating cash flow starts from the lower profit and adds the 10 of depreciation back, so CFO rises by 2.5: the tax saving. On the balance sheet, PP&amp;E is now 90, cash is 2.5 higher than it would otherwise be, and retained earnings are 7.5 lower. Assets fell by 7.5 net, equity fell by 7.5. It balances.</p><p>Do the same with a credit sale, a loan and an inventory write-down. Interviewers ask exactly these, and the habit of tracing every event through all three statements is what makes you fast at spotting what does not fit.</p>`],
 ['The checks every analyst runs',`<p>Four roll-forwards catch most errors and many games.</p><p><b>Cash:</b> opening cash plus the net change in the cash flow statement must equal closing cash on the balance sheet. <b>Retained earnings:</b> opening plus profit after tax minus dividends equals closing, give or take items through other comprehensive income. <b>Fixed assets:</b> opening PP&amp;E plus capex minus depreciation minus disposals equals closing. <b>Debt:</b> opening plus new borrowing minus repayments equals closing.</p><p>When a roll-forward does not close, the gap is in a note: an acquisition, a revaluation, a currency effect, a reclassification. Finding it is the work. When the gap is large and unexplained, that is a finding in itself.</p>`],
 ['How to read an Indian annual report',`<p>Use the <b>consolidated</b> statements; the standalone accounts leave out subsidiaries, which is where problems are often kept. Read the <b>auditor’s report</b> before the numbers: look for qualifications, an emphasis of matter, and the key audit matters, which tell you where the auditor spent time. Then read the notes on related party transactions, contingent liabilities, segment reporting and the revenue and receivables breakdown.</p><p>Finally, compare three to five years side by side. One year tells you a level; five tell you a direction, and accounting problems almost always show up as a direction.</p>`]],
 ex:'Kaveri Pumps (invented), one year, ₹ crore\n\nP&L                          Cash flow\nRevenue          1,000       Profit before tax        150\nEBITDA             220       + Depreciation            40\nDepreciation       −40       + Interest paid           30\nEBIT               180       − Rise in receivables    −45\nInterest           −30       − Rise in inventory      −20\nPBT                150       + Rise in payables        15\nTax (25%)          −38       − Tax paid               −38\nPAT                112       CFO                      132\n                             Capex                    −90\nChecks                       Free cash flow (simple)   42\nCFO ÷ EBITDA = 60%           Dividends paid           −30\nCFO ÷ PAT    = 118%          Interest paid            −30\nRetained earnings: +112 − 30 = +82',
 cq:'A company books a ₹50 crore sale on credit on the last day of the year. With a 25% tax rate and no cost of goods, what happens to profit, receivables and operating cash flow this year?',
 ca:'Profit after tax rises by 37.5 and receivables rise by 50. Operating cash flow starts from the higher profit before tax (+50), subtracts the rise in receivables (−50) and pays 12.5 more tax, so CFO falls by 12.5. Profit up, cash down: that gap is what the next lesson is about.',
 videos:[{t:'The Ultimate Guide to Financial Statements',ch:'Accounting Stuff',id:'eorpdJUWfTA',why:'A clear walk through all three statements and how they connect. Watch it after the section on one transaction.'}],
 links:[{t:'Corporate filings and announcements',src:'BSE India',u:'https://www.bseindia.com/corporates/ann.html',why:'Download any listed company’s annual report and practise the four roll-forwards.'}]},

{t:'Earnings quality: when profit and cash disagree',min:20,
 sum:'Accruals, working capital and the red flags that tell you reported profit may not last, with the research behind them.',
 key:'Profit made of cash is worth more than profit made of accruals. When earnings grow faster than operating cash flow for more than a year or two, the market eventually notices, and the research says it usually notices late.',
 secs:[
 ['Profit is an opinion, cash is a fact',`<p>Accrual accounting is necessary: a builder who finishes half a bridge has done half the work even if the client has not paid. But every accrual is an estimate, and estimates are where judgment, optimism and occasionally fraud enter.</p><p>Define <b>accruals</b> simply as the difference between profit and operating cash flow. A company with ₹100 crore of profit and ₹95 crore of CFO has small accruals. One with ₹100 crore of profit and ₹20 crore of CFO has booked ₹80 crore of profit it has not collected, usually as receivables, inventory or capitalised costs.</p>`],
 ['What the research found',`<p>In 1996 Richard Sloan showed that the cash part of earnings is more persistent than the accrual part. Companies whose earnings were mostly accruals saw those earnings fall back in later years, and investors did not seem to anticipate it: a portfolio buying low-accrual companies and selling high-accrual ones earned large abnormal returns in the following year. This became known as the <b>accrual anomaly</b>, and it has been studied in many markets since.</p><p>Three years later Messod Beneish built a model from eight ratios to estimate the probability that a company was manipulating earnings. It correctly flagged a large share of known manipulators before they were caught. Its ratios are a useful checklist even if you never compute the score.</p>`],
 ['Working capital, in days',`<p>Most accrual problems live in working capital, and the clearest way to read it is in days.</p><p><b>Receivable days</b> = trade receivables ÷ revenue × 365. <b>Inventory days</b> = inventory ÷ cost of goods sold × 365. <b>Payable days</b> = trade payables ÷ cost of goods sold × 365. The <b>cash conversion cycle</b> is receivable days plus inventory days minus payable days: how long a rupee is tied up between paying a supplier and collecting from a customer.</p><p>A company growing 20% a year with a 90-day cycle needs to fund extra working capital equal to about a quarter of its incremental revenue. That is why fast-growing distributors and contractors often report rising profits and falling cash. Growth itself is not a red flag. Days rising while growth is flat is.</p>`],
 ['The red flags, with reasons',`<p><b>Receivables growing faster than revenue.</b> Either collection is slowing or sales are being booked early. Beneish calls this the days-sales-in-receivables index.</p><p><b>Capital work in progress that keeps growing.</b> Costs moved into CWIP do not hit the P&amp;L until the asset is commissioned.</p><p><b>Falling depreciation rate</b>, or a change in useful lives. Stretching asset lives lifts profit without any change in the business.</p><p><b>Other income doing the heavy lifting</b>, and <b>exceptional items</b> that appear every year.</p><p><b>Large related party transactions</b>: sales to, loans to, or purchases from entities the promoter controls. Read the note.</p><p><b>Auditor changes</b>, qualifications, or a resigning independent director with a pointed letter.</p><p><b>Promoter share pledges</b>, disclosed in the shareholding pattern. Pledged shares can be sold by lenders if the price falls, and a promoter under pressure has a reason to keep the price up.</p>`],
 ['The Beneish ratios',`<p>Each ratio compares this year with last year. <b>DSRI</b>, receivables to sales. <b>GMI</b>, gross margin deterioration. <b>AQI</b>, the share of assets that are neither current assets nor plant, which rises when costs are capitalised. <b>SGI</b>, sales growth itself, because high growth creates pressure to keep it up. <b>DEPI</b>, a falling depreciation rate. <b>SGAI</b>, selling and admin costs relative to sales. <b>LVGI</b>, leverage. <b>TATA</b>, total accruals relative to assets.</p><p>Practitioners usually treat a combined score above about −1.78 as a warning. Use it as a screen that tells you where to read, never as proof. Many companies that score badly are simply growing fast or changing business mix.</p>`],
 ['A routine you can run in ten minutes',`<p>For any company, put five years side by side and compute: CFO ÷ EBITDA, CFO ÷ PAT, receivable days, inventory days, CWIP ÷ gross block, other income ÷ PBT and the effective tax rate. For a healthy industrial business, CFO ÷ EBITDA over several years is usually 70 to 90 percent. Anything persistently far from that needs an explanation you can write down in one sentence.</p>`]],
 ex:'Nimbus Textiles (invented), ₹ crore         FY24     FY25\nRevenue                                 800    1,040   (+30%)\nTrade receivables                       160      272   (+70%)\nReceivable days                          73       95\nPAT                                      64       88\nCFO                                      70       18\nCFO ÷ PAT                              109%      20%\n\nRead: profit up 38%, cash down 74%. Sales grew 30% but\nreceivables grew 70%: the extra ₹112 crore of receivables\nis most of the gap. Next step: the receivables ageing note\nand the top customers. Are any related parties?',
 cq:'A company’s receivable days rise from 60 to 85 while revenue growth slows from 25% to 8%. Name two explanations and the note you would read to tell them apart.',
 ca:'Either customers are paying more slowly (stress among customers, or looser credit terms to keep sales up), or revenue is being recognised early or is not genuine. Read the receivables ageing schedule and the related party note: overdue buckets growing points to collection problems, large balances with related parties points to something worse.',
 papers:[{t:'Do Stock Prices Fully Reflect Information in Accruals and Cash Flows about Future Earnings?',a:'Richard G. Sloan',y:1996,src:'The Accounting Review',u:'https://www.cuhk.edu.hk/acy2/workshop/June2009Wasley/1996TAR).pdf',why:'The paper behind the accrual anomaly. Read the introduction and the first results table.'},
         {t:'The Detection of Earnings Manipulation',a:'Messod D. Beneish',y:1999,src:'Financial Analysts Journal',u:'https://rpc.cfainstitute.org/research/financial-analysts-journal/1999/the-detection-of-earnings-manipulation',why:'Where the eight ratios and the M-score come from. Short and practical.'}]},

{t:'Returns on capital: is this a good business?',min:21,
 sum:'ROE, ROCE and ROIC, how to compute them properly, and why growth only creates value when returns beat the cost of capital.',
 key:'A business creates value only when it earns more on its capital than that capital costs. Growth at returns below the cost of capital destroys value, however good it looks in the headlines.',
 secs:[
 ['The one comparison that matters',`<p>Every business raises money from shareholders and lenders and invests it in plants, inventory, software and people. The question is how much profit each rupee of that capital earns, and whether that beats what investors could earn elsewhere at similar risk: the <b>cost of capital</b>.</p><p>If a company earns 25% on its capital and its cost of capital is 12%, each rupee it reinvests creates value. If it earns 9%, each rupee it reinvests is worth less than a rupee the moment it is spent. This is why two companies growing at the same rate can deserve very different valuations.</p>`],
 ['Three ratios and what each one hides',`<p><b>Return on equity</b> (ROE) = profit after tax ÷ average shareholders’ equity. It is what shareholders earn, but it rises with leverage: borrow more, shrink equity, and ROE goes up even if the business is no better.</p><p><b>Return on capital employed</b> (ROCE) = EBIT ÷ (equity + debt), or EBIT ÷ (total assets − current liabilities). It removes the leverage effect, and is the ratio most Indian brokers publish. It is pre-tax and usually includes cash, which flatters cash-poor companies and penalises cash-rich ones.</p><p><b>Return on invested capital</b> (ROIC) = NOPAT ÷ invested capital, where NOPAT is EBIT × (1 − tax rate) and invested capital is net fixed assets plus operating working capital plus goodwill. It strips out excess cash and investments, so it measures only the operating business. It takes longer to compute, which is exactly why it is worth computing.</p>`],
 ['Taking ROIC apart',`<p>ROIC = NOPAT margin × capital turnover. A consumer company with a 15% margin and capital turning 3 times a year earns 45%. A cement company with a 15% margin and capital turning 0.8 times earns 12%. Same margin, different businesses.</p><p>The DuPont identity does the same for equity: ROE = net margin × asset turnover × assets ÷ equity. When ROE improves, ask which of the three moved. Margin and turnover improvements are operating; leverage improvements are financial and carry risk.</p>`],
 ['Growth, reinvestment and value',`<p>Growth is not free. To grow, a company must reinvest part of its profit, and the amount depends on its returns: <b>growth = reinvestment rate × return on new capital</b>. A company earning 20% on new capital needs to reinvest 40% of NOPAT to grow 8%. One earning 10% must reinvest 80%.</p><p>That leads to the value formula from McKinsey’s valuation work: value = NOPAT × (1 − g ÷ ROIC) ÷ (WACC − g). Read it slowly. When ROIC equals WACC, growth adds nothing: the extra profit is exactly offset by the reinvestment needed. When ROIC is below WACC, faster growth lowers value. The worked example below makes this concrete.</p>`],
 ['Returns fade, and moats slow the fade',`<p>High returns attract competitors, and over time most companies’ returns drift toward the average. Michael Mauboussin’s work on corporate performance shows this fade clearly in large samples, and also shows that some companies resist it for long periods. The rest of this archive is about why.</p><p>Robert Novy-Marx found in 2013 that gross profitability, gross profit divided by assets, predicts stock returns about as well as cheapness does. Productive assets are a signal the market underprices. It is one more reason to put returns on capital at the centre of your work.</p>`],
 ['How to compute it from an annual report',`<p>Take EBIT from the P&amp;L, excluding other income. Apply the effective tax rate, or the statutory rate if the effective one is distorted. For invested capital, take net block plus CWIP plus right-of-use assets plus goodwill, add inventory and receivables, subtract payables and other operating liabilities, and leave out cash and treasury investments. Use the average of opening and closing capital. Compute five years. The level tells you about the business; the trend tells you about management.</p>`]],
 ex:'Same growth, different returns. NOPAT 100, WACC 12%, growth 8%\nValue = NOPAT × (1 − g ÷ ROIC) ÷ (WACC − g)\n\nROIC 30%   100 × (1 − 0.27) ÷ 0.04   = 1,833\nROIC 12%   100 × (1 − 0.67) ÷ 0.04   =   833\nROIC 10%   100 × (1 − 0.80) ÷ 0.04   =   500\n\nNo growth at all: 100 ÷ 0.12           =   833\n\nAt ROIC = WACC, growth is worth nothing.\nBelow it, growth destroys value.',
 cq:'A company raises ROE from 14% to 19% in two years. Margins and asset turnover are unchanged. What happened, and is it good news?',
 ca:'Leverage rose: assets ÷ equity went up by about a third, so the company borrowed more or bought back shares. The business is no better, shareholders now carry more risk, and the higher ROE should not command a higher multiple on its own.',
 papers:[{t:'The Other Side of Value: The Gross Profitability Premium',a:'Robert Novy-Marx',y:2013,src:'Journal of Financial Economics (NBER working paper version)',u:'https://www.nber.org/papers/w15940',why:'Shows profitable firms earn higher returns, with a measure simple enough to compute yourself.'},
         {t:'Measuring the Moat: Assessing the Magnitude and Sustainability of Value Creation',a:'Michael J. Mauboussin and Dan Callahan',y:2016,src:'Morgan Stanley Investment Management (Consilient Observer)',u:'https://www.morganstanley.com/im/publication/insights/articles/article_measuringthemoat.pdf',why:'Section one on value creation and the fade of returns is the best primer there is.'}],
 links:[{t:'How are companies valued?',src:'McKinsey Explainers',u:'https://www.mckinsey.com/featured-insights/mckinsey-explainers/how-are-companies-valued',why:'A short read on growth, ROIC and value from the authors of the standard valuation textbook.'}],
 videos:[{t:'Valuation seminar, day 1 afternoon (part 2)',ch:'Aswath Damodaran',id:'MlHPAnFp3uo',why:'Covers cash flows, growth and terminal value, including why growth must be paid for with reinvestment.'}]},

{t:'Moats: why some returns last',min:19,
 sum:'The structural reasons a few businesses keep earning high returns, how to see them in the numbers, and how they erode.',
 key:'A moat is not a good product or a strong brand on its own. It is a structural reason competitors cannot copy what makes the business profitable, and you should be able to see it in returns that stay high through a full cycle.',
 secs:[
 ['From high returns to lasting returns',`<p>The last lesson showed that value depends on returns above the cost of capital, and that most returns fade. The investor’s real question is therefore not how good a business is today, but how long its advantage will last. That length is what the market is paying for when it gives one company a P/E of 60 and another a P/E of 12.</p>`],
 ['Five sources of advantage',`<p><b>Intangible assets.</b> Brands that let a company charge more for the same physical product, licences and approvals that limit who can compete, and patents. A brand counts only if it raises price or lowers the cost of winning customers.</p><p><b>Switching costs.</b> When changing supplier is costly, risky or tedious, customers stay even when a rival is cheaper. Core banking software and enterprise systems are classic examples.</p><p><b>Network effects.</b> The product becomes more valuable as more people use it: exchanges, payment networks, marketplaces. These tend toward one or two winners.</p><p><b>Cost advantages.</b> Scale, process or location. A cement plant next to its limestone quarry and close to its market has a freight advantage no competitor can copy without the same geography.</p><p><b>Efficient scale.</b> A market only large enough for one or two profitable players, such as a regional pipeline or an airport. Entry would make returns inadequate for everyone, so nobody enters.</p>`],
 ['Porter’s five forces, briefly',`<p>Michael Porter’s framework asks who captures the value an industry creates. Rivalry among existing players, the threat of new entrants, the bargaining power of suppliers, the bargaining power of buyers, and the threat of substitutes. An industry where all five are weak is a good industry to be in; a company with a moat is one that keeps those forces weak around itself even when its industry is hard.</p>`],
 ['Seeing a moat in the numbers',`<p>Look for <b>ROIC above the cost of capital across a full cycle</b>, not in one good year. Look for <b>gross margin that holds when input costs spike</b>, which is pricing power in action. Look for <b>stable or rising market share</b> without rising advertising or discounts. And look at <b>incremental returns</b>: does new capital earn as much as old capital, or is the company expanding into weaker territory?</p><p>Numbers alone are not enough. A temporarily high return from a commodity upcycle looks like a moat for two years. You need a sentence that explains why competitors cannot simply copy the business, and that sentence should survive a conversation with a competitor’s salesperson.</p>`],
 ['How moats erode',`<p>Technology changes the cost of switching or the economics of distribution. Regulation opens a licensed market. A new entrant with deep pockets is willing to lose money for years. Management spends the cash a moat produces on acquisitions in businesses without one. Watch for falling incremental returns, rising customer acquisition costs and a gross margin that no longer recovers after a cost spike.</p>`],
 ['Questions to write down for any company',`<p>Who are the customers, and what would it cost them to switch? What would a well-funded entrant need to replicate the business, and how long would it take? What happened to margins the last time input costs rose sharply? Where does the company put its free cash flow, and what do those investments earn? If you cannot answer, that is what to ask on the next earnings call.</p>`]],
 ex:'Input costs rise 20% for two paint makers (invented), ₹ crore\n\n                       Brandco         Smallco\nRevenue before           1,000           1,000\nMaterials before (55%)     550             550\nMaterials after (+20%)     660             660\nPrice increase taken        12%              3%\nRevenue after            1,120           1,030\nGross margin before        45%             45%\nGross margin after         41%             36%\n\nBrandco passed most of the cost on and kept customers.\nSmallco could not. That difference is the moat, measured.',
 cq:'A company’s ROIC has been 35% for three years, during which the price of its main product doubled because of a global shortage. Is that a moat?',
 ca:'Not yet. Three years inside one price cycle cannot separate a structural advantage from a favourable cycle. Look at returns in the last downturn, its position on the industry cost curve, and whether competitors earned similar returns. If everyone earned 35%, the shortage is the cause, not a moat.',
 papers:[{t:'Measuring the Moat: Assessing the Magnitude and Sustainability of Value Creation',a:'Michael J. Mauboussin and Dan Callahan',y:2016,src:'Morgan Stanley Investment Management',u:'https://www.morganstanley.com/im/publication/insights/articles/article_measuringthemoat.pdf',why:'The checklist sections on industry analysis and firm-specific advantage turn this lesson into a working method.'},
         {t:'The Five Competitive Forces That Shape Strategy',a:'Michael E. Porter',y:2008,src:'Harvard Business Review',u:'https://hbr.org/2008/01/the-five-competitive-forces-that-shape-strategy',why:'Porter’s own restatement of the framework, with examples.'}],
 videos:[{t:'The Little Book that Builds Wealth',ch:'Pat Dorsey, Talks at Google',id:'YFS5JBgz1Xc',why:'The former Morningstar head of equity research on the sources of moats and the traps that look like moats.'}]},

{t:'DCF from first principles',min:24,w:'dcf',
 sum:'Free cash flow, the discount rate and the terminal value, built step by step, with the consistency checks that separate a defensible DCF from a spreadsheet.',
 key:'A DCF is a story about the business, written in numbers. Every input should trace back to something you believe about volumes, prices, costs and reinvestment, and the growth you assume must be paid for with the reinvestment you forecast.',
 secs:[
 ['The idea',`<p>A business is worth the cash it will hand its capital providers in future, discounted to today at a rate that reflects the risk of that cash. Everything else in valuation is either a shortcut for this or a way to get the inputs right.</p><p>The standard version values the whole firm: forecast <b>free cash flow to the firm</b> (FCFF), discount it at the <b>weighted average cost of capital</b> (WACC), add a <b>terminal value</b> for the years beyond the forecast, and you have enterprise value. Subtract net debt and minority interests and add non-operating investments to reach the value of the shares.</p>`],
 ['Free cash flow to the firm',`<p>FCFF = EBIT × (1 − tax rate) + depreciation − capital expenditure − increase in net working capital.</p><p>Notice what is missing: interest. FCFF is the cash before anything is paid to lenders or shareholders, because the discount rate will account for both. Forecast it from drivers, not from last year’s growth: volume × price for revenue, cost per unit for margins, capacity additions for capex, and days for working capital. A cement company’s revenue is tonnes sold × realisation per tonne; its capex is the cost per tonne of capacity it adds.</p>`],
 ['The discount rate',`<p>WACC = (E ÷ V) × cost of equity + (D ÷ V) × cost of debt × (1 − tax rate), using market values where you can.</p><p>The <b>cost of equity</b> is usually estimated as risk-free rate + beta × equity risk premium. For an Indian company valued in rupees, the risk-free rate is the current yield on the 10-year government bond. The <b>equity risk premium</b> is the extra return investors demand for owning equities; Aswath Damodaran publishes implied estimates for India and other markets, including a country risk component. <b>Beta</b> measures how much the stock moves with the market; use an industry average of unlevered betas, relevered to the company’s own debt, rather than a noisy regression on one stock.</p><p>The <b>cost of debt</b> is what the company would pay to borrow today, not the coupon on old loans. Interest is tax-deductible, hence the (1 − tax rate).</p>`],
 ['Terminal value, and why it dominates',`<p>Beyond the explicit forecast, assume cash flow grows at a constant rate forever: terminal value = FCFF in the first year after the forecast ÷ (WACC − g). This figure is often 60 to 80 percent of the total value, so the growth rate deserves more care than any single forecast year.</p><p>Two rules keep it honest. First, <b>g cannot exceed the long-run growth of the economy</b>, and a common discipline is to cap it at the risk-free rate. Second, <b>growth must be funded</b>: the reinvestment in the terminal year should equal g ÷ return on new capital. If you assume 6% growth forever with almost no capex, you have assumed infinite returns on capital.</p>`],
 ['From enterprise value to a share price',`<p>Enterprise value minus borrowings and lease liabilities, plus cash and non-operating investments, minus minority interests and any other claims such as employee options or large contingent liabilities you expect to crystallise, gives equity value. Divide by diluted shares outstanding.</p>`],
 ['Testing it',`<p>A single number from a DCF is false precision. Build a table of value against WACC and terminal growth, one or two points either way, and report the range. Then ask what has to be true for the bull case: what margins, what market share, what returns on capital. If those assumptions would make this company better than the best company in its industry has ever been, the bull case is a fantasy.</p><p>Finally, compare implied multiples. If your DCF says a commodity business is worth 30 times earnings, either you have found something or you have made a mistake, and it is usually the second.</p>`]],
 ex:'Deccan Cement (invented), ₹ crore. WACC 12%, terminal g 5%\n\nYear                    1      2      3      4      5\nEBIT × (1 − 25%)      300    330    360    390    420\n+ Depreciation        120    130    140    150    160\n− Capex              −180   −190   −200   −200   −210\n− Rise in NWC         −20    −20    −20    −20    −20\nFCFF                  220    250    280    320    350\nDiscount factor      0.893  0.797  0.712  0.636  0.567\nPresent value         196    199    199    204    199\n\nSum of PVs                                    997\nTerminal value 350 × 1.05 ÷ (0.12 − 0.05) = 5,250\nPV of terminal value 5,250 × 0.567        = 2,977\nEnterprise value                            3,974\nLess net debt                                −800\nEquity value                                3,174\nTerminal value share of EV                    75%',
 cq:'In the worked example, the analyst raises terminal growth from 5% to 7% but leaves terminal capex unchanged. What is wrong?',
 ca:'Growth must be paid for. At 7% growth and, say, a 14% return on new capital, half of terminal NOPAT must be reinvested. Leaving capex unchanged assumes the extra growth arrives free, which inflates the terminal value twice: higher growth and lower reinvestment.',
 papers:[{t:'Equity Risk Premiums (ERP): Determinants, Estimation and Implications',a:'Aswath Damodaran',y:2022,src:'NYU Stern working paper, updated every year',u:'https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/papers/ERP2022Formatted.pdf',why:'The reference on where the equity risk premium comes from and how to estimate it, including for emerging markets. Read sections one and four.'}],
 videos:[{t:'Valuation seminar, day 1 afternoon (part 1)',ch:'Aswath Damodaran',id:'xf3HTN5qH9U',why:'Risk-free rates, equity risk premiums and the cost of capital, from the person who wrote the textbook.'},
         {t:'Valuation seminar, day 1 afternoon (part 2)',ch:'Aswath Damodaran',id:'MlHPAnFp3uo',why:'Cash flows, growth and terminal value: the second half of this lesson, at lecture length.'}],
 links:[{t:'Damodaran Online: data',src:'NYU Stern',u:'https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/',why:'Industry betas, country risk premiums and the implied equity risk premium, updated regularly.'}]},

{t:'Multiples, and what the price already assumes',min:20,w:'priced',
 sum:'Why multiples are compressed DCFs, which ones work best, and how to reverse a valuation to see what the market expects.',
 key:'Every multiple hides a forecast. A P/E of 50 is not expensive or cheap on its own; it is a statement about growth, returns and risk. Turn the multiple back into those assumptions and you can argue with them.',
 secs:[
 ['A multiple is a DCF in disguise',`<p>Take the steady-state value formula and divide by earnings, and you get the <b>justified P/E</b>: P/E = (1 − g ÷ ROE) ÷ (cost of equity − g). With ROE of 20%, growth of 8% and a cost of equity of 13%, the justified forward P/E is (1 − 0.4) ÷ 0.05 = 12. Raise ROE to 30% and it becomes 14.7; raise growth to 10% and it becomes 16.7.</p><p>This is why multiples should never be compared without asking about returns and growth. Two companies on the same P/E can be very differently priced once those are taken into account.</p>`],
 ['Which multiple, when',`<p><b>P/E</b> is about equity and is affected by leverage and by one-off items in profit. <b>EV/EBITDA</b> compares the whole firm with operating profit before depreciation, so it works across different capital structures and is common for capital-intensive businesses, but it ignores the capex needed to keep EBITDA coming. <b>EV/EBIT</b> or <b>EV/NOPAT</b> fixes that. <b>P/B</b> is the natural multiple for banks and insurers, where book value is mostly financial assets; its justified form is (ROE − g) ÷ (cost of equity − g). <b>EV per tonne</b> or per unit of capacity is used for cement and other commodities, compared with the cost of building new capacity.</p>`],
 ['What the research says',`<p>Jing Liu, Doron Nissim and Jacob Thomas compared how well different multiples explain actual prices across thousands of US companies. Multiples based on <b>forward earnings forecasts</b> performed best, and their accuracy improved the further ahead the forecasts reached. Multiples based on historical cash flows, book value and sales performed worst. The practical lesson is to use forward earnings where good forecasts exist, and to be suspicious of sales multiples except for businesses with no profits yet.</p>`],
 ['Choosing peers',`<p>Peers should share growth, returns and risk, not just an industry label. A premium two-wheeler maker and a commuter-scooter maker are both automobile companies with very different economics. Adjust for differences you can measure, and when a company trades at a large premium to its peers, find the assumption that explains it before deciding it is expensive.</p>`],
 ['Reverse the valuation',`<p>Instead of forecasting and computing a value, start from today’s price and ask what forecast it implies. This is the core of Mauboussin and Rappaport’s expectations investing: the market price is a set of expectations about sales growth, margins and the length of the competitive advantage. Your job is to decide whether those expectations are too high or too low, which is easier than producing an exact value.</p><p>The CementCo widget below does exactly this: it turns a market value into the volume the company would need to sell, and compares it with its capacity.</p>`],
 ['Traps',`<p>Cyclical companies look cheapest at the top of the cycle, when earnings are at a peak, and most expensive at the bottom. Holding companies trade at discounts that rarely close. One-off gains flatter trailing multiples. Different depreciation and lease accounting make EV/EBITDA comparisons across countries unreliable. And a low multiple is often a correct forecast of a weak future, not a mistake by the market.</p>`]],
 ex:'Justified P/E = (1 − g ÷ ROE) ÷ (cost of equity − g)\nCost of equity 13%\n\nROE 20%, g 8%    (1 − 0.40) ÷ 0.05 = 12.0×\nROE 30%, g 8%    (1 − 0.27) ÷ 0.05 = 14.7×\nROE 20%, g 10%   (1 − 0.50) ÷ 0.03 = 16.7×\nROE 30%, g 10%   (1 − 0.33) ÷ 0.03 = 22.2×\n\nReverse it: a stock on 22× with a 13% cost of equity\nneeds about 30% ROE and 10% growth for a long time.',
 cq:'A steel maker trades at 5× this year’s earnings, its lowest multiple in a decade, after record profits. Cheap?',
 ca:'Probably not. Steel earnings are cyclical, and a low multiple on peak profits is the market pricing in a fall. Value it on mid-cycle earnings, or on EV per tonne against replacement cost, before calling it cheap.',
 papers:[{t:'Equity Valuation Using Multiples',a:'Jing Liu, Doron Nissim and Jacob Thomas',y:2002,src:'Journal of Accounting Research',u:'https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/pubfiles/318/Equity_Valuation_Using_Multiples.pdf',why:'The evidence on which multiples work. The summary in the introduction is enough to change how you choose them.'}],
 videos:[{t:'Valuation seminar, day 2 afternoon (part 2)',ch:'Aswath Damodaran',id:'qtLlhg71r4g',why:'From the afternoon session on stories and numbers, valuation as a craft and pricing companies.'}]},

{t:'Reading a bank, and why every sector needs its own lens',min:23,
 sum:'How banks make money and how they fail, the ratios Indian bank analysts use, how to value them, and the key metrics for other sectors.',
 key:'For a bank, debt is raw material, not financing. So enterprise value and EBITDA mean nothing, and the questions become: how cheaply does it borrow, how well does it lend, and how much capital does it hold against being wrong?',
 secs:[
 ['Why banks break the usual tools',`<p>An industrial company borrows to fund its business. A bank’s business is borrowing, from depositors, and lending at a higher rate. Interest is its main cost of goods sold, not a financing cost. That is why you cannot compute a meaningful enterprise value or EBITDA for a bank, and why bank analysis works with equity measures: book value, return on equity and price to book.</p><p>Banks also fail differently. A manufacturer usually declines slowly. A bank can look healthy until loans it made two or three years earlier go bad together, and by then its capital may not cover the losses.</p>`],
 ['The bank P&L',`<p><b>Net interest income</b> (NII) is interest earned on loans and investments minus interest paid on deposits and borrowings. Divide it by average interest-earning assets to get the <b>net interest margin</b> (NIM), the spread the bank earns. <b>Other income</b> is fees, commissions and treasury gains; fees are durable, treasury gains are not.</p><p>Subtract operating expenses to get <b>pre-provision operating profit</b> (PPOP). The <b>cost-to-income ratio</b>, opex ÷ (NII + other income), measures efficiency. Then come <b>provisions</b> for expected loan losses. Provisions ÷ average loans is the <b>credit cost</b>, the single number that moves bank profits most across a cycle.</p>`],
 ['The bank balance sheet',`<p>On the liability side, deposits matter most, and cheap deposits matter most of all. The <b>CASA ratio</b>, current and savings account deposits as a share of total deposits, measures how much of the bank’s funding costs it little. A high CASA ratio is one of the few durable advantages in Indian banking.</p><p>On the asset side, <b>advances</b> are loans. The <b>credit-deposit ratio</b> shows how much of its deposits the bank has lent. <b>Capital adequacy</b>, measured by the CRAR and the CET1 ratio under the RBI’s Basel III rules, shows how much loss it can absorb.</p>`],
 ['Asset quality',`<p>A loan becomes a <b>non-performing asset</b> when interest or principal is overdue for more than 90 days. <b>Gross NPA</b> is bad loans as a share of total loans; <b>net NPA</b> is after provisions already made. The <b>provision coverage ratio</b> (PCR) is provisions ÷ gross NPA: how much of the bad book is already written down. The <b>slippage ratio</b>, new NPAs in a period ÷ opening standard loans, is the leading indicator. Watch restructured loans and special mention accounts too, which are early stages of stress.</p>`],
 ['From ROA to ROE to value',`<p>Banks earn thin returns on assets and use leverage to turn them into returns on equity. ROE = ROA × assets ÷ equity. A bank with a 1.5% ROA and 8 times leverage earns 12% on equity; at 1.8% ROA it earns 14.4%.</p><p>Value it on price to book. The justified P/B is (ROE − g) ÷ (cost of equity − g). A bank that sustainably earns 18% with a 13% cost of equity and 10% growth is worth (0.18 − 0.10) ÷ (0.13 − 0.10) = 2.7 times book. One that earns 12% is worth 0.7 times book. This is why Indian banks trade at such different multiples: the market is pricing their sustainable ROE, which depends mostly on funding cost and credit cost.</p>`],
 ['Other sectors, other lenses',`<p><b>Cement and commodities:</b> capacity, utilisation, realisation per tonne, cost per tonne and the industry cost curve. Value on EV per tonne against the cost of new capacity, and on mid-cycle earnings. <b>IT services:</b> constant-currency revenue growth, deal wins and their total contract value, utilisation, attrition and margins against wage inflation and currency. <b>Consumer staples:</b> volume growth versus price growth, gross margin against raw material prices, distribution reach and advertising as a share of sales. The method is the same everywhere: find the two or three drivers that explain most of the profit, and read the company through them.</p>`]],
 ex:'Tapti Bank (invented), ₹ crore\n\nAverage interest-earning assets   1,00,000\nNet interest income                  3,600   NIM 3.6%\nOther income                         1,200\nOpex                                 2,400   cost-to-income 50%\nPPOP                                 2,400\nAverage loans                       70,000\nProvisions                             700   credit cost 1.0%\nPBT                                  1,700\nPAT (25% tax)                        1,275\nAverage total assets              1,10,000   ROA 1.16%\nAverage equity                      10,000   ROE 12.8%\n\nJustified P/B, cost of equity 13%, g 9%\n(0.128 − 0.09) ÷ (0.13 − 0.09) = 0.95× book\n\nIf credit cost fell to 0.5%: PAT 1,538, ROE 15.4%,\njustified P/B 1.6×. Credit cost is the whole debate.',
 cq:'Two banks both report a 16% ROE. Bank A has an ROA of 2.0%, Bank B an ROA of 1.0%. Which would you pay more book value for, and why?',
 ca:'Bank A. Its ROE comes from earning more on its assets with leverage of 8 times; Bank B needs 16 times leverage to reach the same ROE, so a smaller rise in bad loans wipes out more of its equity. The same ROE carries very different risk.',
 papers:[{t:'Valuing Financial Service Firms',a:'Aswath Damodaran',y:2009,src:'NYU Stern working paper',u:'https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/country/finsvce.pdf',why:'Why standard DCF fails for banks, and how dividend, FCFE and excess-return models fix it.'}],
 videos:[{t:'Valuation seminar, day 2 morning (part 1)',ch:'Aswath Damodaran',id:'T_gegih_20A',why:'From the session covering the dark side of valuation, young and declining companies, and valuing banks.'},
         {t:'Valuation seminar, day 2 morning (part 2)',ch:'Aswath Damodaran',id:'landwqMR3AA',why:'The second half of the same session.'}],
 links:[{t:'Database on Indian Economy',src:'Reserve Bank of India',u:'https://dbie.rbi.org.in/',why:'Official series on credit growth, deposit rates and bank asset quality to put any bank in context.'}]},

{t:'From annual report to stock pitch',min:22,
 sum:'The research routine, building a variant view, earnings season, how an initiation note is structured, and how to pitch a stock in two minutes.',
 key:'A pitch is not a summary of the company. It is a claim that the market is wrong about something specific, the evidence for it, what will make the market see it, and what would prove you wrong.',
 secs:[
 ['The research routine',`<p>Start with primary sources. Read three to five years of <b>annual reports</b>, including the notes and the management discussion. Read the last four <b>earnings call transcripts</b> and <b>investor presentations</b>, all filed on the BSE and NSE websites. Read the <b>shareholding pattern</b> for promoter holding and pledges, and any credit rating rationales, which often explain a company’s weaknesses more plainly than the company does.</p><p>Then build a small model with the drivers from earlier lessons, and write down the three questions the numbers raise. Those questions are what you take to the company, to its competitors, its suppliers and its customers. Industry conversations are where most real insight comes from.</p>`],
 ['Consensus, and the variant view',`<p><b>Consensus</b> is the average forecast of the analysts who cover a stock. It is roughly what the price already reflects. A <b>variant view</b> is a specific, evidenced disagreement with it: consensus expects 8% volume growth, you expect 15% because two competitors are shutting capacity.</p><p>A variant view needs four things: what the market believes, what you believe, why you believe it, and the <b>catalyst</b> that will make the market change its mind, such as a results season, a capacity announcement or a regulatory decision. Without a catalyst you may be right for years without being paid.</p>`],
 ['Earnings season',`<p>Before results, write a <b>preview</b>: your estimates, consensus, and the two or three numbers that will move the stock. After results, write a <b>review</b> within hours: what was reported, how it compared, what management said on the call, and whether your thesis is stronger or weaker. Then revise your estimates. Over time, the record of what you expected and what happened is the best training you will get.</p>`],
 ['Anatomy of an initiation note',`<p>The first page carries the rating, the target price, the investment thesis in three points, and the key financials. The body explains each thesis point with evidence, then the industry, the company’s position in it, the financial model and its key assumptions, the valuation with a sensitivity table, and the risks to the view. In India, SEBI’s Research Analyst Regulations govern published research, including disclosures about conflicts of interest.</p>`],
 ['How to pitch in two minutes',`<p>Open with the recommendation and the number: buy, with a target 30% above today’s price over twelve months. Give two or three thesis points, each with a number the listener can check. State the valuation and what it implies. Name the catalyst. Name the main risk and what would make you change your mind. Stop.</p><p>Interviewers test whether you know the business beyond the pitch: be ready for the follow-up on the bear case, the accounting, the peer valuation and what consensus expects. A pitch you cannot defend for ten minutes is a pitch you should not give.</p>`],
 ['What actually matters in the job',`<p>Lawrence Brown, Andrew Call, Michael Clement and Nathan Sharp surveyed and interviewed hundreds of sell-side analysts. Industry knowledge ranked as the most important determinant of analysts’ pay, and private communication with management was a more useful input than many public sources. The lesson for a student is that depth beats breadth: know one industry well enough that its executives find you worth talking to.</p>`]],
 ex:'Two-minute pitch, Deccan Cement (invented)\n\nBuy, target ₹540 vs ₹410 today (+32%), 12 months.\n\n1. Consensus has volumes +7% next year. Two rivals in its\n   region are shutting 4 mt of old kilns (filings, Q2 calls).\n   We expect +12%.\n2. Its new plant sits on its own limestone: cost per tonne\n   ₹350 below the regional average.\n3. Net debt falls from 2.1× to 1.2× EBITDA by FY27.\n\nValuation: 11× FY27 EV/EBITDA, peers 12–14×.\nEV per tonne $95 vs ~$120 replacement cost.\nCatalyst: Q3 results and the capacity closures in March.\nRisk: a price war if a national player enters the region.\nWrong if: regional prices fall >5% for two quarters.',
 cq:'Your pitch says a company will beat consensus earnings by 15%, but you cannot name an event in the next year that will show it. What is missing, and why does it matter?',
 ca:'A catalyst. Without an event that forces the market to update, the gap between your view and consensus can persist indefinitely, and the stock can stay mispriced long after you are proven right on paper.',
 papers:[{t:'Inside the "Black Box" of Sell-Side Financial Analysts',a:'Lawrence D. Brown, Andrew C. Call, Michael B. Clement and Nathan Y. Sharp',y:2015,src:'Journal of Accounting Research',u:'https://ideas.repec.org/a/bla/joares/v53y2015i1p1-47.html',why:'A survey of what analysts actually do, value and get paid for. The most useful career reading in this curriculum.'}],
 videos:[{t:'Valuation seminar, day 2 afternoon (part 1)',ch:'Aswath Damodaran',id:'G_XDRDD0GSs',why:'From the afternoon session on stories and numbers and valuation as a craft: how a narrative becomes a valuation.'}],
 links:[{t:'NSE India',src:'National Stock Exchange',u:'https://www.nseindia.com/',why:'Filings, call transcripts and shareholding patterns for the research routine.'},{t:'SEBI',src:'Securities and Exchange Board of India',u:'https://www.sebi.gov.in/',why:'The Research Analyst Regulations that govern published research in India.'}]}
];
